---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import FaithfulnessCheckDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/FaithfulnessCheckDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "45-evaluation";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<BaseLayout title="45 · Evaluation" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Module 35 gave you recall@k and precision@k against a handful of hand-checked queries. Evaluation turns that
        habit into a system: a golden dataset, automated metrics, and a gate in CI — so "the new prompt feels better"
        becomes a number that either passed or didn't.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Golden datasets</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>golden dataset</strong> is 20+ labeled question/answer pairs, ideally drawn from real user questions
      plus the Module 10 documents. Each row needs a question, reference context (which chunks should be retrieved),
      and a reference answer — the fixture every later metric compares against.
    </p>

    <h2>RAGAS-style metrics</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Four metrics, split across the retrieval/generation boundary so a regression points at the actual cause:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">FAITHFULNESS</span>
        <p>Every claim in the answer is checked against the retrieved context — catches hallucination even when the answer sounds confident.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">ANSWER RELEVANCE</span>
        <p>Does the response actually address the question asked, independent of whether it's grounded?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">CONTEXT PRECISION</span>
        <p>Were the retrieved chunks actually relevant?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">CONTEXT RECALL</span>
        <p>Were the relevant chunks retrieved at all?</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Together they separate "retrieval failed" from "generation failed" instead of one blended verdict.</p>

    <FaithfulnessCheckDemo />

    <h2>Evaluating agent trajectories, not just answers</h2>
    <p>
      Faithfulness and context recall score a single answer. Module 85's agent loop needs a different question: was
      the <strong>trajectory</strong> — the sequence of tool calls it actually made — correct, not just the final
      output? A trajectory eval checks whether the agent called the right tools, in a sane order, without redundant
      calls, and stopped once the task was actually done.
    </p>
    <p>
      This needs its own golden set: each row is a task plus the expected tool-call sequence, or an acceptable set of
      sequences since more than one valid path often exists. An agent can produce a perfectly faithful final answer
      while still failing the trajectory eval — it called an expensive or side-effecting tool it never needed, which
      the answer alone won't show you.
    </p>

    <h2>LLM-as-judge and calibration</h2>
    <p>
      Most of these metrics are themselves computed by asking another LLM to score the response — an
      <strong>LLM-as-judge</strong>. That's cheap and scales to hundreds of examples, but a judge model has its own
      blind spots. Calibrate it: periodically compare judge scores against human ratings on the same examples, and
      fix the judge prompt or model if they disagree often — before trusting it to gate anything.
    </p>

    <h2>Regression evals in CI</h2>
    <p>
      Once you trust the metrics, wire them into CI as an <strong>eval-gated deploy</strong>: every change to the
      prompt, retrieval pipeline, or model re-runs the golden dataset, and the build fails if faithfulness or context
      recall drops below threshold. Same discipline as a unit test suite, aimed at model quality instead of code
      correctness — it's what turns Module 55's traces from "interesting logs" into "the thing that stops a bad
      release."
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Trusting LLM-as-judge scores without ever checking them against human labels — an uncalibrated judge can be confidently wrong.</li>
      <li>Building a golden dataset once and never updating it as real user questions drift from your original assumptions.</li>
      <li>Blending faithfulness and context recall into one "quality score" — you lose the ability to tell whether retrieval or generation caused a regression.</li>
      <li>Scoring only an agent's final answer — a faithful answer can still hide an unnecessary or side-effecting tool call the trajectory eval would have caught.</li>
      <li>Adding an eval gate to CI but never lowering the threshold as the pipeline improves, so it stops catching anything.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</BaseLayout>
